    key: ["Req ↔ design ↔ code ↔ test case ↔ result", "Forward: every req is covered", "Backward: every test/code has a reason", "Consistency: content matches, not just a link", "Maintain continuously, re-check on every change"],
    answer: "Traceability means every requirement links to the design elements, code and test cases that implement and verify it, and to the test results. <strong>Bidirectional</strong> means you can go both ways. Forward from a requirement, to see it's covered. Backward from a test or a piece of code, to see which requirement justifies it. That catches orphan tests and extra work nobody asked for. <strong>Consistency</strong> is the part people forget: the link must be correct in content, so the test really checks what the requirement says, at the current version. In my AUTOSAR Classic role I owned requirement analysis, traceability and requirement-to-test mapping for MCAL and BSW validation. My habits were: link at the moment I create a test case, not at the end; tag test results with requirement IDs so reports can be generated; and when a requirement changes, run an impact check on every linked test. The tool we used was [fill: requirement/ALM tool used, e.g. DOORS, Polarion, codebeamer], and coverage gaps were reviewed [fill: how often / in which review].",
    followups: ["What happens to traceability when a requirement is deleted?", "How would you automate a coverage check?"]
